  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nMesoanalysis this morning depicts an enhanced wave situated\nacross the northern Plains with its attendant cold front\ncrossing into northern Kansas. This cold front will continue to\nmove southward throughout the day and eventually slow and\nperhaps stall across the forecast area. The boundary will serve\nas a focus for scattered thunderstorms later this afternoon and\nthis evening. Have pushed POPs a tad earlier than NBM had given\nto account for HREF members indicating development between\n21-23z. Environment will be conducive for at least isolated \nsevere thunderstorm potential given steep low-level lapse rates,\nincreased 0-6km bulk shear of 30-40 knots, and MLCAPE 2500-3000\nJ/kg along and ahead of the boundary. This environment would\nyield the potential for isolated instances of damaging wet \nmicrobursts and severe hail. PWATs will also be around \n1.3-1.5\", which is exceeding the 90th percentile for today, so \nheavy rainfall will also be a concern. Severe weather risk will \ndiminish into the mid to late evening, with storms still a \npossibility into the late evening and overnight, mainly across \neastern zones.\n\nHot temperatures remain in the forecast for the next few days.\nToday, south of the cold front will climb into the mid to upper\n90s, though north of the boundary will be in the 80s. Everywhere\nacross the forecast area will get back into the 90s and even a\nfew 100s by Sunday and Monday. Monday looks to be the hottest\nday with widespread upper 90s to around 100 for the entire area.\nTuesday will be the beginning of more seasonable temperatures,\nalthough the area will be split once again by a cold front. \nCurrent forecast has the front positioned from Larned, through \nDodge City, and down toward Liberal. Area south of that axis \nwill remain in the 90s and close to 100 across south-central \nKansas, whereas areas north will be in the upper 70s to mid 80s.\nIt is not until Tuesday where everyone is seeing reprieve from \nthe heat with continued seasonable temperatures for the rest of\nthe week. Lows will generally remain in the mid to upper 60s to\nlow 70s to start the forecast period, but some 50s and low 60s \ncould work their way into late week next week. Precipitation \nchances remain elevated in the forecast nearly every day with \nsome varying level of chance and placement through the forecast \nperiod.",
